307:We conclude this section with the following two theorems that relate existence criteria between several fault-tolerant variants of ICs.
308-
309:\begin{theorem}\label{theo:kreg-ic-iff-redic}
310-If $G$ is $k$-regular for $k \ge 2$, then IC exists if and only if RED:IC exists.
311:\end{theorem}
312-\begin{proof}
313-We know that existence of RED:IC implies existence of IC, so we need only show the converse.
314-Let $G$ be a $k$-regular graph for which IC exists, and let $u,v \in V(G)$ be distinct vertices; we will show $S = V(G)$ is a RED:IC.
315-Because $deg(u) = deg(v) = k$, we know $|(N[u] \cap S) \triangle (N[v] \cap S)| = |N[u] \triangle N[v]| = (k+1) + (k+1) - 2|N[u] \cap N[v]| = 2j$ for some $j \in \mathbb{N}_0$.
316-We know $j \neq 0$ because IC is assumed to exist, so $j \ge 1$, meaning all vertices are at least 2-distinguished.
317-Because $G$ is $k$-regular for $k \ge 2$, every vertex is at least 2-dominated; thus, $S$ is a RED:IC, completing the proof.
318-\end{proof}
319-
320:\begin{theorem}
321-If $G$ is $k$-regular for $k \ge 2$, then DET:IC exists if and only if ERR:IC exists.
322:\end{theorem}
323-\begin{proof}
324-Similar to Theorem~\ref{theo:kreg-ic-iff-redic}, we will show that existence of DET:IC implies existence of ERR:IC.
325-Let $G$ be a $k$-regular graph which has DET:IC, and let $u,v \in V(G)$ be distinct vertices; we will show $S = V(G)$ is an ERR:IC.
326-From the proof of Theorem~\ref{theo:kreg-ic-iff-redic}, we know $|(N[v] \cap S) \triangle (N[u] \cap S)| = 2j$ for some $j \in \mathbb{N}_0$.
327-We know $j \neq 0$ because DET:IC exists.
328-We also know $j \neq 1$ because $deg(u) = deg(v)$ and $j=1$ would imply that $u$ and $v$ are only 2-distinguished rather than the $2^\#$-distinguishing required by DET:IC.
329-Thus, $j \ge 2$, meaning all vertices are at least 3-distinguished, and all vertices are at least 3-dominated because $k \ge 2$.
330-Therefore, $S$ is an ERR:IC, completing the proof.
331-\end{proof}
