333:\section{NP-completeness of ERR:IC}\label{sec:npc}
334-
335:Many graphical parameters related to detection systems, such as finding optimal IC, LD, or OLD sets, are NP-complete problems \cite{ld-ic-np-complete-2, NP-complete-ic, NP-complete-ld, old}.
336:We will show that ERR-IC, the problem of determining the smallest ERR:IC set, is also NP-complete.
337:For additional information about NP-completeness, see Garey and Johnson \cite{np-complete-bible}.
338-
339-Clearly, ERR-IC is in NP, as every possible candidate solution can be generated nondeterministically in polynomial time, and each candidate can be verified in polynomial time using Theorem~\ref{theo:erric-char}.
340:To show that ERR-IC is NP-complete, we will demonstrate a reduction from 3-SATISFIABILITY (3SAT) to ERR-IC.
341-
342-\npcompleteproblem{3SAT}{Let $X$ be a set of $N$ variables.
343-Let $\psi$ be a conjunction of $M$ clauses, where each clause is a disjunction of three literals from distinct variables of $X$.}{Is there is an assignment of values to $X$ such that $\psi$ is true?}
344-
345-\npcompleteproblem{Error-correcting Identifying Code (ERR-IC)}{A graph $G$ and integer $K$.}{Is there an ERR:IC set $S$ with $|S| \le K$? Or equivalently, is ERR:IC($G$) $\le K$?}
