463:\subsection{Lower Bound on ERR:IC(G) for Cubic Graphs}
464-
465-For a dominating set $S \subseteq V(G)$ of $G$ and a vertex $v \in S$, Slater \cite{ftld} defines the \emph{share} of $v$ to be $sh(v) = \sum_{u \in N[v]}{1/|N[u] \cap S|}$; that is, $v$'s contribution to the domination of its neighbors.
466-Each vertex $u \in V(G)$ with $|N[u] \cap S| = k$ contributes $\frac{1}{k}$ to $sh(x)$ for each $x \in N[u] \cap S$ (and $0$ to any other vertex).
467-Therefore, because $S$ is a dominating set, $\sum_{v \in S}{sh(v)} = n$, implying that the inverse of the average share is equal to the density of $S$ in $V(G)$.
468:Therefore, an upper bound on the average share (over all detectors) can be reciprocated to give a lower bound for the density.
469-As a shorthand, we will let $\sigma_A$  denote $\sum_{k \in A}{\frac{1}{k}}$ for some sequence of single-character symbols, $A$.
470-Thus, $\sigma_a = \frac{1}{a}$, $\sigma_{ab} = \frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{b}$, and so on.
471-We also let $dom(v) = |N[v] \cap S|$ denote the \emph{domination number} of some vertex $v \in V(G)$.
472-
473:\begin{theorem}\label{theo:erric-cubic-lower}
474:If $G$ is a cubic graph, then $\textrm{ERR:IC\%}(G) \ge \frac{5}{6}$.
475:\end{theorem}
476-\begin{proof}
477-Let $S$ be an ERR:IC for $G$.
